message:
Hold the shadow to the gaps a mutant would have walked through

Each of these would have survived the tests as they stood:

- a client handed out while the checkpoint is still loading, so every
  step of the wait would be logged as a timeout;
- p50 as a mean, where one cold question speaks for a hundred warm ones
  (it is p50Of now, and tested as a median);
- questions put together inside observe() itself - the fake server
  could not see that, because a request reaches it after the step has
  returned either way, so a client that notes calls as they are made
  does;
- a step with no successor never written until quit, and a verdict
  given to a step whose successor came minutes later when a timer ran
  late;
- rows reaching the file in the order their questions finished rather
  than the order of the steps;
- stop() that only asks: a laya-serve wedged in a forward pass would
  outlive the app, so stop now insists after a grace a test can shorten;
- the broker's events never reaching the shadow in createApp, and the
  app closing without taking laya-serve down - both now end to end,
  with a real permission card answered over RPC.
